.TH CHTTPCLIENT 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
chttpclient \- overview of the HTTP/1.1 client of the C Collections Library
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
chttpclient sends HTTP/1.1 requests and reads the responses. It can use
plain TCP, TLS or a Unix domain socket. chttpclient.h includes chttp.h.
chttp.h holds the types that the client shares with the server:
  - chttp_method_t.
  - chttp_request_body_t and its CHTTP_*_BODY macros.
  - chttp_tls_config_t.
  - The CHTTP_STATUS_* constants.
  - The base64 helpers and the Basic auth helpers.

Link a program that uses the client with \-lccollections. The library
itself depends on OpenSSL (libssl and libcrypto). A link against the
shared library therefore needs nothing more. For a static link, get the full
list from pkg-config:

    cc app.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs --static ccollections)

You can build the library without the client (make
WITH_CHTTPCLIENT=0). A program that uses the client then does not link.
.fi
.SS The client handle
.nf
chttpcli is an opaque VALUE handle. It is a uint64_t, not a pointer.
Do not cast it to or from a pointer. Do not use it as an address.
CHTTPCLI_INVALID is 0. It is the chttpcli equivalent of NULL. Therefore,
"if (!cli)" tests if there is a client.

Each call resolves the handle before it uses the client. The call therefore
finds and refuses a stale handle, that is, a handle whose client was
destroyed. The result of a stale handle is:
  - ccol_invalid_args from a function.
  - NULL from a function that returns a future.
  - A fatal error from chttpclient_destroy.
A stale handle never causes a read of freed memory.

ccol_create_chttpclient and ccol_create_chttpclient_mp make a client.
chttpcli_construct, chttpcli_construct_scoped, chttpcli_declare and
chttpcli_declare_scoped are wrappers around them. chttpclient_destroy
destroys a client.

chttp_default_client gives one client for the full process. chttp_get,
chttp_post, chttp_put, chttp_delete, chttp_patch, chttp_do and
chttp_run_query use that client. The library owns that client. Do not
destroy it.
.fi
.SS Requests
.nf
chttp_request_t holds a method, a URL, a body and a set of headers.
chttp_request_new and chttp_request_new_mp copy the URL and the body.
The caller can therefore free its own buffers immediately. The fields method,
url, body, expect_continue and prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect are
public. Use chttp_request_set_header and chttp_request_get_header to
set and read the headers. chttp_request_free frees the request.
.fi
.SS Responses
.nf
chttpcli_response has four public fields:

  status_code  the HTTP status of the final response.
  body         a heap buffer that ends with a NUL, or NULL.
  body_len     the number of bytes in body before that NUL.
  headers      a chmap(char * -> char *) from each lower-case field
               name to its value.

body is NULL and body_len is 0 in these cases:
  - A body of zero length, for example a 204, a response to HEAD or a
    "Content-Length: 0".
  - All streaming requests. Their body goes to the write callback.
Therefore, before you read body, make sure that body != NULL or
body_len > 0. Do this also after a success.

The server can send a name on more than one line. That name maps to the
values joined with ", ". Set-Cookie is the exception: it maps to its
first value. See chttpclient_resp_header(3). chttpclient_resp_free
frees the response. A response does not depend on the client that made
it. You can free it before or after chttpclient_destroy.

Each entry point that takes a resp_out sets *resp_out to NULL before it
does other work. It keeps *resp_out NULL on each return that is not a
success. chttpclient_resp_free(NULL) does nothing. A call to it after
such a request is therefore always safe.
.fi
.SS Three ways to run a request
.nf
Tier 1 is chttpclient_do and chttpclient_do_streaming. It runs the
request on the calling thread. That thread blocks until the request
ends. The pool size of the client (chttpclient_set_pool_size) sets the
maximum number of Tier 1 requests that run at the same time. A caller
above that maximum waits for a slot.

Tier 2 is chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_async_streaming. It
gives the request to a shared reactor engine and returns a ctpool_future
immediately. chttpclient_async_result_get waits for the result.

Tier 3 is chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming. It
submits the request through the same engine and blocks until the
request ends. Its calls have the same shape as the calls of Tier 1. Many
callers then share the few threads of the engine. Each request does not
block a thread of its own.

The engine of Tiers 2 and 3 is one engine for each process. All clients
share it. It starts on first use. It stops automatically when no request
is in progress and no connection is in the pool. Use
chttpcli_set_engine_logger, chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs and
chttpcli_set_engine_num_reactor_threads to configure it. It is separate
from the reactor of chttpserver.

All three tiers apply the same rules to these items: URLs, redirects,
headers, TLS, "Expect: 100-continue", interim responses and limits. All
three tiers give the same result codes.
.fi
.SS Connection reuse
.nf
After a response on a keep-alive connection, the client keeps that
connection in an idle pool. The key of the pool is the origin:
  - "scheme://host:port". The host comparison ignores case.
  - "unix://<path>" for a Unix domain socket.
An HTTPS connection keeps its TLS session. A later request to the same
origin then does not do DNS, the TCP handshake or the TLS handshake.

The pool has a maximum for each origin and a maximum in total. An idle
connection expires after a short time. Above a maximum, the client
closes a finished connection. It does not put the connection in the
pool.

Before the client reuses a connection from the pool, it checks the
connection. The peer can close the connection, or send data on it while
it is idle. The client then closes that connection and uses a different
one. This has no cost to the caller. Bytes that arrive after a complete
response answer no request. The client never reads them as the
response to the next request.

A reused connection can be dead, and the client can find this only
after it writes the request. If the client read nothing back on that
connection, it sends the request one more time, on a new connection.
The client never does this after it sent a body that a "100 Continue"
confirmed. In that case, the server accepted the body. A second send
can then run a request that is not idempotent two times.

The client never keeps these connections:
  - A connection whose response started before the client sent the
    full request.
  - A connection whose request had an "Expect: 100-continue" that got
    a final status as the answer.
  - A connection whose HTTP/1.0 response has Transfer-Encoding. RFC 9112
    section 6.1 calls that framing faulty.
chttpclient_set_tls closes all idle HTTPS connections of the client.
The reason is that the pool key does not include the TLS settings of a
handshake.
.fi
.SS Unix domain sockets
.nf
A URL of the form "http+unix://<percent-encoded socket path>/path?query"
gets to a server on a Unix domain socket. This works in all tiers and
with keep-alive pooling.

Percent-encode each byte of the socket path that is not an RFC 3986
unreserved character. This includes '/'. For example, "/run/app.sock"
becomes "%2Frun%2Fapp.sock".

The Host header is "localhost" by default, as with curl --unix-socket.
A request can set its own Host header. A path that is too long for
sockaddr_un.sun_path gives ccol_http_invalid_url. On Linux, that limit
is 108 bytes, with the terminating NUL.

There is no "https+unix://". chttpclient_set_tls has no effect on such
a request. A URL of this form has no credentials. A redirect can never
move a request to this transport, or from one socket to a different
socket; see chttpclient_do(3).
.fi
.SS Default request headers
.nf
The client adds a value for these headers:
  - Host.
  - Accept ("*/*").
  - User-Agent.
  - Content-Length and Content-Type, from the body.
  - Authorization, from credentials in the URL.
  - Expect, from expect_continue.
The client does not add a header when the request sets that header. The
name comparison ignores case. The client sends a header that the request
sets exactly as given. Only POST, PUT and PATCH have a body; see
chttp_request_new_mp(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A TLS connection can renegotiate (TLS 1.2) or get a KeyUpdate
(TLS 1.3) during a request. The connection must then read before it can
write, or write before it can read. Each tier waits for the direction
that the connection asks for. Such a connection therefore continues to make
progress.

The client opens each socket close-on-exec. It also opens each file
that its TLS layer reads close-on-exec. A process that the application
starts (system, popen, posix_spawn, fork and exec) therefore gets no
connection.

The client never changes the disposition of SIGPIPE. None of its writes
causes SIGPIPE; see chttpclient_do(3).

There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with the client:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a client and before the first
    request of Tier 2 or Tier 3. Each process then makes its own
    clients.
  - Call fork(2), and then call exec(3) in the child immediately, as
    popen and system do. This is supported at all times, whatever the
    client does.
A child that continues with no exec(3) while a client from before the
fork is live is not supported. The reason is that fork(2) does not copy
the threads of the client and its engine.

A process can destroy all clients that used Tier 2 or Tier 3 before it
forks. The child of that process can then use both tiers. The child
starts its own engine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Each function that takes a chttpcli is thread-safe. Many threads can
run requests on one client at the same time. They can also change its
settings at the same time.

chttpclient_destroy can run while other threads use the client. It
waits for the requests that are in progress. Two destroys of one handle
that overlap are a fatal error. The application must therefore serialize
the destroy. The destroy must be the last thing that any thread does
with that handle.

A chttp_request_t and a chttpcli_response have no lock. A thread that
changes one of them must have exclusive access. A thread that reads one
of them while a different thread changes it must also have exclusive
access.
.fi
